    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The coarse scale flow properties of strike slip faults in porous sandstone are computed from detailed field measurements. The faults, which appear in the Valley of Fire State Park, Nevada, were previously characterized and the flow properties of their individual features were estimated (Myers and Aydin, in press). The faults formed from the shearing of joint zones and are composed of a core of fine grain fault rock and a damage zone of joints and sheared joints. High resolution fault zone maps and permeability data, estimated using image analysis calibrated to actual measurements, are incorporated into numerical procedures to determine the permeability of coarse blocks comprising portions of the fault zone. The methodology can provide accurate upscaled description of the fault zone at whatever level of resolution is required.\r\n\r\nMaps of upscaled permeability indicate that these faults have a strongly anisotropic permeability structure. Regardless of the mean orientation of high permeability features, the principal directions of the overall fault zone permeability correspond to the mean fault core direction for all faults considered. For all faults considered the fault zone permeability normal to the fault strike is about two orders of magnitude less than the permeability of the host rock. Although increased slip leads to a higher density of shear induced joints, the permeability parallel to the fault strike decreases with increased slip over the range of slip considered here. This is because increased slip leads to a high variation in the orientation of the high permeability features and also produces significant low permeability features that are interspersed with the more highly permeable features. The procedures developed and applied to the faults formed from shearing across joint zones in sandstone in this study can, however, be used for any type of fault for which detailed structural and permeability data are available or can be estimated.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
